<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arica is a port city in the far north of Chile, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Long part of Peru and an outlet for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and trade from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, it was occupied by Chile during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> after the storming of the Morro de Arica in 1880. Its fate was disputed for decades until the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> of 1929 gave Arica to Chile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> to Peru.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arica.jpg" alt="Arica" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: B1mbo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_de_Arica.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
